    "seo": {
      "title": "Southern Iranian Cuisine: Gulf Fish Stews, Shrimp and Dates",
      "description": "Discover the documented Gulf-coast repertoire of southern Iran through tamarind fish stew, Bushehr’s shrimp dishes, and date-palm food history."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "coastal-introduction",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Begin with the coast",
        "content": "Southern cuisine is a broad label. This account begins with documented foods of the Persian Gulf coast, especially Bushehr, rather than assuming that one coastal menu represents the whole south. Visit Iran associates ghalieh mahi with Khuzestan, Bushehr, and Hormozgan; ToIran’s Bushehr guide likewise places fish and shrimp dishes prominently in its account of the city. Together, these sources provide a useful coastal introduction while leaving substantial inland and southeastern traditions outside its coverage. [s1][s3]\n\nThe relationship between the sea and the table also reaches inland. A research article on southern Fars examines edible Gulf fish used in Mohr and Lamerd counties. Its evidence draws on fishing-dock cargoes, markets, and interviews with fishermen and fish sellers. This documents a food-supply connection across administrative boundaries, rather than a cuisine confined to settlements directly beside the water. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "ghalieh",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The sour, herbal character of ghalieh",
        "content": "Ghalieh mahi is a fish khoresh, or stew. Visit Iran’s description brings together coriander, fenugreek, garlic, onion, pepper, turmeric, and tamarind. The tamarind supplies sourness, while the herbs and garlic form a substantial part of the preparation. Rice is a usual accompaniment, although bread is also recorded. The resulting flavor profile is more specific than the general description “spicy southern food.” [s1]\n\nThe same source lists several possible fish and notes shrimp as an alternative. These choices matter: a dish name does not identify a single species or guarantee an identical preparation. Its assertion that the method is uniform should not obscure the limits of a short tourism description; it supplies an example rather than a comparative survey of household recipes. [s1]\n\nBushehr’s repertoire also includes qalieh meigoo, a sour shrimp stew, and meigoo polow, rice mixed with shrimp and herbs. ToIran describes dal adas as a lentil preparation with garlic, onion, potato, and tomato paste, served with bread. These examples introduce contrasting formats—stew, mixed rice, and lentils—within the city’s food landscape. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "dates-and-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Dates and the limits of a regional portrait",
        "content": "Date palms add a different historical dimension. Encyclopaedia Iranica assembles evidence for their economic and cultural importance and records travelers’ descriptions of date cultivation in southern Iran. Its discussion of Marco Polo’s account of Hormoz links dates with salted fish, offering a historical glimpse of foods associated with the coast. Such testimony describes a particular observer and period; it should not be treated as a current dietary survey. [s4]\n\nFor food travel, these sources suggest an approach centered on named preparations and ingredients. Comparing fish stew, shrimp rice, and a lentil dish reveals more than ordering an unspecified “southern specialty.” A fuller regional account still requires locally grounded evidence from beyond Bushehr and the Gulf fish repertoire, particularly for island, inland, and southeastern cooking.",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s4"]
      }
    ],
