    "description": "Mashhad’s sholeh, a thick preparation of meat, grains, and pulses, introduces the communal dimension of Khorasan’s food traditions. Saffron farming around Gonabad connects the region’s food identity with irrigation, agricultural knowledge, and contemporary questions of authenticity. [s1][s3][s4]",
    "tags": ["Khorasan", "Mashhad", "Sholeh", "Saffron", "Gonabad", "Food heritage"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T22:47:14Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T22:47:14Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Khorasan Cuisine: Mashhad’s Sholeh and Saffron Landscapes",
      "description": "An evidence-based introduction to Mashhad’s communal sholeh, Khorasan food souvenirs, Gonabad’s saffron farming, and authenticity concerns."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "two-entry-points",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From communal cooking to the farming landscape",
        "content": "A useful introduction to Khorasan’s food begins in two settings: the shared cooking pot and the agricultural landscape. Visit Iran describes Mashhad’s sholeh as a substantial food associated with collective occasions. FAO’s account of Gonabad, meanwhile, explains how saffron cultivation operates within a landscape dependent on carefully managed water. Together, these examples connect food with both social practice and the work required to produce ingredients. [s1][s3]\n\nThey do not constitute a complete regional menu. The strongest culinary sources inspected here concern Mashhad and Razavi Khorasan, while FAO’s contemporary saffron work also identifies North and South Khorasan as producing provinces. The page therefore distinguishes a documented introduction from a comprehensive account of all three provinces. [s2][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "sholeh",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Sholeh Mashhadi and the occasion of eating",
        "content": "Sholeh Mashhadi is a thick, long-cooked preparation classified by Visit Iran as a kind of ash, the Iranian family of substantial soups. Its description combines meat, bulgur wheat, rice, and several pulses with spices that can include ginger, cardamom, and pepper. It compares the texture with halim, another thick preparation of meat and grain, while distinguishing their ingredients. [s1]\n\nThe occasion is central to understanding the dish. Visit Iran associates sholeh with religious commemorations and other communal events, describing a preparation demanding considerable time and resources. Its account records service with a little gheimeh, a separate stew, and sangak bread. This is a description of a local serving tradition, not a promise that every portion or venue will follow it. [s1]\n\nA broader provincial guide also identifies rice dishes, local stews, saffron, and barberries within Razavi Khorasan’s food and souvenir repertoire. These additional examples help prevent sholeh’s ceremonial prominence from standing in for everything people cook or eat. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "saffron-landscape",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Saffron beyond the souvenir packet",
        "content": "At Gonabad, FAO describes a saffron farming system supported by qanats, underground water channels used for irrigation. Its account places cultivation within an arid and semiarid environment where water scarcity shapes agricultural choices. The significance of saffron here extends beyond seasoning: it links a valuable crop with local livelihoods and inherited knowledge about managing a difficult environment. [s3]\n\nThis perspective gives food travel an agricultural dimension. A packet bought in a city can be considered alongside the water, cultivation, and processing behind it. FAO identifies tourism as an opportunity associated with the farming system, but its designation alone does not establish that a particular farm offers public visits or a scheduled visitor program. [s3]\n\nAuthenticity is a contemporary concern. In September 2025, FAO announced collaboration with Mashhad University of Medical Sciences on saffron testing and post-harvest guidance, including work intended for producing provinces across Khorasan. The announcement identifies adulteration and inconsistent handling as problems. It documents planned work rather than demonstrating that fraud has subsequently been eliminated. [s4]\n\nSholeh and saffron thus offer complementary starting points: one directs attention to collective preparation and occasions, the other to farming and product integrity. Further research is needed to extend this portrait across Khorasan’s distinct local and community traditions.",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3", "s4"]
      }
    ],
